If the galvanometer placed between $F$ and $H$ indicates no
 current, either transient or permanent, then by (9) and (10),
 since $H-F=0$,

$$\begin{equation*}
P x=Q y ; \tag{11}
\end{equation*}$$

and

$$\begin{equation*}
L \frac{d x}{d t}+M\left(\frac{d x}{d t}+\frac{d y}{d t}\right)=0, \tag{12}
\end{equation*}$$

whence

$$\begin{equation*}
L=-\left(1+\frac{P}{Q}\right) M \tag{13}
\end{equation*}$$


Since $L$ is always positive, $M$ must be negative, and therefore
 the current must flow in opposite directions through the coils
 placed in $P$ and in $B$. In making the experiment we may
 either begin by adjusting the resistances so that

$$\begin{equation*}
P S^{\prime}=Q R, \tag{14}
\end{equation*}$$

which is the condition that there may be no permanent current,
 and then adjust the distance between the coils till the galvano
meter ceases to indicate a transient current on making and
 breaking the battery connexion; or, if this distance is not
 capable of adjustment, we may get rid of the transient current
 by altering the resistances $Q$ and $S$ in such a way that the ratio
 of $Q$ to $S$ remains constant.
